## Proof

We determine whether $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}}$, $f(t)=(\sin(xt))_{x\in\mathbb{R}}$, is continuous when $\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}}$ carries the product topology.

**Key fact (universal property of the product topology).** Let $\{Y_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ be a family of topological spaces and $Y=\prod_{\alpha\in A} Y_\alpha$ the product space with the product topology. A map $g:Z\to Y$ is continuous **if and only if** every coordinate projection $\pi_\alpha\circ g:Z\to Y_\alpha$ is continuous, for all $\alpha\in A$.

This is a standard characterization: the product topology is the coarsest (initial) topology making all projections continuous, so a map into the product is continuous exactly when it is continuous in every coordinate.

**Apply to our setting.** Here $Y_\alpha = \mathbb{R}$ for every $\alpha\in A=\mathbb{R}$, and $Z=\mathbb{R}$. For each fixed $x\in\mathbb{R}$, the $x$-th coordinate of $f$ is
$$
(\pi_x\circ f)(t) = \sin(xt), \qquad t\in\mathbb{R}.
$$
For each fixed $x$, the map $t\mapsto xt$ is continuous (scalar multiplication), and $\sin:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is continuous. Being a composition of continuous functions, $t\mapsto\sin(xt)$ is continuous for every $x\in\mathbb{R}$.

Since **every** coordinate function $\pi_x\circ f$ is continuous, by the universal property of the product topology, $f$ is continuous.

**Conclusion.** The function $f$ **is continuous**.

